# <%= current_page.data.title %>

If you have an RDS instance on Modernisation Platform and a namespace on a Container Platform
cluster, you can use a port-forward pod on the Container Platform to reach that
database and prove connectivity.

There are two parts to it:

1. Allow your cluster's address range through the database's security group. This is a
   Terraform change in your Modernisation Platform namespace.
2. Connect to the database. To do this from your own machine, use a port-forward pod.

## Before you start

You need:

- a namespace on a Container Platform nonlive cluster.
  See [Deploying an application to the Container Platform](https://user-guide.development.container-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/documentation/getting-started/1-deploying-an-application-to-the-container-platform.html)
- an RDS instance on the Modernisation Platform. See [Creating resources in the Modernisation Platform](https://user-guide.modernisation-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/user-guide/creating-resources.html)
- the ability to raise a pull request against
  [modernisation-platform-environments](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments)

## Step 1: allow your cluster to reach the database

Find the security group config in your Modernisation Platform RDS code (or add it if not already present). For example:

```hcl
resource "aws_security_group" "rds-example" {
  description = "RDS Security Group"
  vpc_id      = data.aws_vpc.shared.id
}
```

Add in the security group rule for the cluster you are connecting from. For example:

```hcl
resource "aws_security_group_rule" "rds_example_ingress_cp3_octo_nonlive" {
  description              = "container-platform-octo-nonlive nodes"
  type                     = "ingress"
  from_port                = 3306
  to_port                  = 3306
  protocol                 = "tcp"
  security_group_id        = aws_security_group.rds-example.id
  cidr_blocks              = ["10.195.48.0/20"]
}
```

You **must** change `cidr_blocks` to your Container Platform cluster:

| Cluster | Address range |
| --- | --- |
| `container-platform-octo-nonlive` | `10.195.48.0/20` |
| `container-platform-hmpps-nonlive` | `10.195.64.0/20` |
| `container-platform-laa-nonlive` | `10.195.80.0/20` |
| `container-platform-cd-nonlive` | `10.195.96.0/20` |

These are the **node** ranges. Your pods have their own addresses, but traffic leaving the
cluster appears to come from the node it is running on, so the node range is what the
security group needs to allow.

More clusters will be added over time. The ranges for every cluster are defined in the
`vpc_cidr` map in
[network/locals.tf](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments/blob/main/terraform/environments/cloud-platform/network/locals.tf),
where `primary` is the node range you need and `secondary` is the pod range.

If your cluster is not listed above, use the `primary` value for it from that file. Ask in
[#container-platform-alpha-users](https://moj.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0BKZHMQNRK)
if you are not sure which applies to you.

Raise a pull request with the changes. Once it is merged, the pipeline applies it.

The example above uses `3306` for MySQL. Use the port for your engine:

| Engine | Port |
| --- | --- |
| PostgreSQL (RDS and Aurora) | `5432` |
| MySQL and MariaDB | `3306` |
| Microsoft SQL Server | `1433` |
| Redis (ElastiCache) | `6379` |

If you use an engine that is not listed, ask in
[#container-platform-alpha-users](https://moj.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C0BKZHMQNRK)
before you start. Traffic between the platforms passes through an inspection firewall that
allows these ports only, and anything else is dropped without an error.

## Step 2: Get Your Target RDS Details

You will need the following details from your Modernisation Platform RDS to connect:

- hostname
- username
- password

## Step 3: Connect

Follow the instructions in - [Connecting to a Remote Database With A Port Forwarding Pod](/documentation/networking/connecting-to-a-remote-database-woth-port-forward.html)